1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,793
Welcome to lectures fifteen and sixteen:

2
00:00:02,793 --> 00:00:05,656
data quality, integration and validation.

3
00:00:05,656 --> 00:00:18,434
We learn the four dimensions of data quality, how to combine data from multiple sources, how to detect duplicates, how to fix inconsistent encoding, and how to write validation rules.

4
00:00:18,434 --> 00:00:23,112
The lab that follows runs all of this on a real merged Airbnb file.

5
00:00:23,912 --> 00:00:26,971
Raw data is collected for another purpose.

6
00:00:26,971 --> 00:00:38,623
It has duplicates, gaps and mixed spellings, hidden sentinel values like zero, nine ninety nine or N A, and often comes as several files with different schemas.

7
00:00:38,623 --> 00:00:41,463
Clean data is shaped for your question:

8
00:00:41,463 --> 00:00:51,004
one row per entity, gaps explained, one spelling, sentinels converted to real missing values, and one integrated, documented table.

9
00:00:51,004 --> 00:00:51,659
The rule:

10
00:00:51,659 --> 00:00:55,592
audit first, fix second, and re-audit after every fix.

11
00:00:56,392 --> 00:00:58,993
Data quality has four dimensions.

12
00:00:58,993 --> 00:00:59,702
Accuracy:

13
00:00:59,702 --> 00:01:02,776
is the value correct in the real world?

14
00:01:02,776 --> 00:01:03,800
Completeness:

15
00:01:03,800 --> 00:01:06,165
is everything we need present?

16
00:01:06,165 --> 00:01:07,110
Consistency:

17
00:01:07,110 --> 00:01:10,105
is one fact stored one way everywhere?

18
00:01:10,105 --> 00:01:11,130
And validity:

19
00:01:11,130 --> 00:01:13,494
does the value obey the rules?

20
00:01:13,494 --> 00:01:19,720
We will look at each in turn, with examples from the New York City Airbnb data.

21
00:01:20,520 --> 00:01:22,419
Accuracy needs a reference.

22
00:01:22,419 --> 00:01:27,273
You check a value against reality, a trusted source, or a peer group.

23
00:01:27,273 --> 00:01:35,292
For example, a private room in Queens listed at ten thousand dollars a night, against a peer median of fifty nine.

24
00:01:35,292 --> 00:01:41,693
A good proxy reference is the median price of listings with the same borough and room type.

25
00:01:41,693 --> 00:01:45,140
Flag suspicious values rather than deleting them:

26
00:01:45,140 --> 00:01:49,783
the true value is unknown, and deleting real rows biases the data.

27
00:01:49,783 --> 00:01:54,144
Decide whether to cap, fix or remove later, once you know why.

28
00:01:54,944 --> 00:01:58,118
Completeness asks how much is missing and why.

29
00:01:58,118 --> 00:02:03,568
Measure the missing share per column, and the share of rows with no gap at all.

30
00:02:03,568 --> 00:02:15,435
In the Airbnb data, twenty point six percent of listings lack a last review date and reviews per month, and only seventy nine point four percent of rows are fully complete.

31
00:02:15,435 --> 00:02:15,987
But why?

32
00:02:15,987 --> 00:02:21,714
Every one of those listings has zero reviews, so the gap is structural, not random.

33
00:02:21,714 --> 00:02:23,162
Coverage matters too:

34
00:02:23,162 --> 00:02:27,992
a column can be fully filled and still never contain January or April.

35
00:02:28,792 --> 00:02:32,602
Consistency means one fact is stored one way everywhere.

36
00:02:32,602 --> 00:02:36,276
Here are the borough labels in the merged Airbnb file:

37
00:02:36,276 --> 00:02:46,345
ten labels for five boroughs, because lowercase copies of Manhattan, Brooklyn, Queens, Bronx and Staten Island sit next to the proper-case versions.

38
00:02:46,345 --> 00:02:52,672
A group by on this column gives ten groups instead of five and splits every statistic in two.

39
00:02:53,472 --> 00:02:56,543
Consistency can break in three directions.

40
00:02:56,543 --> 00:02:57,421
Across rows:

41
00:02:57,421 --> 00:02:59,687
one borough with two spellings.

42
00:02:59,687 --> 00:03:00,784
Across columns:

43
00:03:00,784 --> 00:03:05,464
the price and price in dollars columns hold the same fact twice.

44
00:03:05,464 --> 00:03:06,853
And across sources:

45
00:03:06,853 --> 00:03:11,387
one team's file says Manhattan while another's says manhattan.

46
00:03:11,387 --> 00:03:16,944
The fix in each case is to choose one standard form and apply it everywhere.

47
00:03:17,744 --> 00:03:25,908
Validity means the value obeys the rules, so write the rules first and test every row against every rule.

48
00:03:25,908 --> 00:03:28,862
Range rules, like price at least zero.

49
00:03:28,862 --> 00:03:33,528
Set rules, like room type being one of three allowed values:

50
00:03:33,528 --> 00:03:37,026
three hundred failures from lowercase labels.

51
00:03:37,026 --> 00:03:38,737
Format and type rules.

52
00:03:38,737 --> 00:03:40,447
A geographic box rule:

53
00:03:40,447 --> 00:03:48,300
five latitude failures, which turned out to be real Staten Island listings, so rules need review too.

54
00:03:48,300 --> 00:03:49,933
And uniqueness rules.

55
00:03:49,933 --> 00:03:50,633
Remember:

56
00:03:50,633 --> 00:03:52,965
validity asks, is it possible?

57
00:03:52,965 --> 00:03:55,220
Accuracy asks, is it correct?

58
00:03:55,220 --> 00:04:00,896
A ten thousand dollar room passes every validity rule and is still wrong.

59
00:04:01,696 --> 00:04:04,149
Data integration has four steps.

60
00:04:04,149 --> 00:04:05,069
Match a key:

61
00:04:05,069 --> 00:04:09,898
find a shared identifier and check it is unique in each source.

62
00:04:09,898 --> 00:04:11,201
Align the schema:

63
00:04:11,201 --> 00:04:15,800
rename columns so both sources use the same names and types.

64
00:04:15,800 --> 00:04:17,180
Resolve conflicts:

65
00:04:17,180 --> 00:04:21,319
where sources disagree, decide which one wins and why.

66
00:04:21,319 --> 00:04:26,608
And re-audit the merged table for duplicates, gaps and new conflicts.

67
00:04:27,408 --> 00:04:37,123
After aligning the schema, merge on the key with an outer join and an indicator column, which shows whether each row is in A only, B only, or both.

68
00:04:37,123 --> 00:04:45,780
In our example forty seven thousand eight hundred ninety five rows are in A only, one thousand are in both, and none are in B only.

69
00:04:45,780 --> 00:04:48,753
Then compare the matched rows field by field:

70
00:04:48,753 --> 00:04:54,899
three hundred borough conflicts, three hundred room type conflicts, and zero price conflicts.

71
00:04:54,899 --> 00:04:59,063
All were case differences only, so we keep source A's spelling.

72
00:04:59,063 --> 00:05:04,680
The result is forty eight thousand eight hundred ninety five rows with no duplicates.

73
00:05:05,480 --> 00:05:07,914
There are three kinds of duplicates.

74
00:05:07,914 --> 00:05:10,957
Exact duplicates have every column identical:

75
00:05:10,957 --> 00:05:12,986
seven hundred rows in the lab.

76
00:05:12,986 --> 00:05:16,840
Same-key duplicates share an I D but other fields differ:

77
00:05:16,840 --> 00:05:22,114
one thousand rows, the seven hundred copies plus three hundred lowercase rows.

78
00:05:22,114 --> 00:05:29,349
And near-duplicates are almost the same after cleaning the text, such as the same host with a similar name:

79
00:05:29,349 --> 00:05:31,716
four hundred eighty one candidates.

80
00:05:31,716 --> 00:05:38,072
Exact duplicates can be dropped automatically; near-duplicates should be reviewed by a person.

81
00:05:38,872 --> 00:05:45,631
To fix inconsistent encoding, strip whitespace, fix the case, and collapse repeated spaces.

82
00:05:45,631 --> 00:05:51,870
For messy codes, use a mapping, so that B K, Bklyn and brooklyn all become Brooklyn.

83
00:05:51,870 --> 00:05:55,138
But standardising can create new duplicates:

84
00:05:55,138 --> 00:06:05,684
after title-casing, the three hundred lowercase rows became exact copies of other rows, which drop duplicates had not been able to see before.

85
00:06:05,684 --> 00:06:09,472
Order matters, so re-run the audit after every fix.

86
00:06:10,272 --> 00:06:13,820
Collect your checks into one reusable rule table.

87
00:06:13,820 --> 00:06:20,191
Range rules like price at least zero and availability between zero and three sixty five.

88
00:06:20,191 --> 00:06:23,015
Type rules like price being an integer.

89
00:06:23,015 --> 00:06:27,866
Uniqueness rules like unique I Ds and no duplicate rows or columns.

90
00:06:27,866 --> 00:06:32,717
And set rules like room type and borough being in the allowed sets.

91
00:06:32,717 --> 00:06:36,772
Write the rules once, and re-run them on every new load.

92
00:06:36,772 --> 00:06:40,320
In the lab, all twelve rules pass after cleaning.

93
00:06:41,120 --> 00:06:45,840
Putting it together, the quality workflow has four stages.

94
00:06:45,840 --> 00:06:46,329
Audit:

95
00:06:46,329 --> 00:06:49,910
shape, types, missing values and duplicates.

96
00:06:49,910 --> 00:06:50,642
Diagnose:

97
00:06:50,642 --> 00:06:55,037
test accuracy, completeness, consistency and validity.

98
00:06:55,037 --> 00:06:55,362
Fix:

99
00:06:55,362 --> 00:06:59,187
integrate sources, deduplicate and standardise.

100
00:06:59,187 --> 00:07:00,245
And validate:

101
00:07:00,245 --> 00:07:08,384
re-run the rules, re-audit, and document what you did, so that someone else can trust and repeat it.

102
00:07:09,184 --> 00:07:10,314
Key takeaways.

103
00:07:10,314 --> 00:07:12,976
Data quality has four dimensions:

104
00:07:12,976 --> 00:07:16,930
accuracy, completeness, consistency and validity.

105
00:07:16,930 --> 00:07:21,772
Validity is checked against rules, accuracy against reality.

106
00:07:21,772 --> 00:07:27,904
Completeness includes why data is missing and which categories never appear.

107
00:07:27,904 --> 00:07:34,036
Integrate sources on a key, align schemas, resolve conflicts, then re-audit.

108
00:07:34,036 --> 00:07:39,604
Standardising can create new duplicates, so re-audit after every fix.

109
00:07:39,604 --> 00:07:43,881
And document the rules and re-run them on every load.

110
00:07:43,881 --> 00:07:45,010
Next, the lab.

111
00:07:45,010 --> 00:07:46,624
Thanks for watching.

